  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 246 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe long term period begins on Monday night with upper level ridging \nextending from the central Gulf Coast towards the Great Lakes \nregion, with an associated broad surface high pressure positioned \nover much of the eastern CONUS. This high pressure regime will keep \na vertically stacked low pressure system just east of the Mid-\nAtlantic and Northeast coast. It will also keep conditions dry, with \nprecipitable water values at or below 0.5 inches and rain chances \nnear 0% across Georgia for the majority of the week. Strong \nradiational cooling overnight will lead to low temperatures in the \nlow to mid 40s on Tuesday morning. With plenty of sunshine and \nincreasing 1000-500 mb thicknesses, temperatures will be on the rise \nthrough midweek. High temperatures on Tuesday will rise into the \nupper 60s to low 70s mid 70s across the majority of the forecast \narea. In spite of the gradual warming trend, morning lows will \nremain brisk each morning, in the 40s with some low 50s creeping in \nby the end of the week. By Thursday and Friday, highs are forecast \nto be in the upper 70s in north Georgia and low 80s in central \nGeorgia. The lack of rain and plenty of sunshine will help dry out \nfiner fuels, so the development of fire danger concerns will need to \nbe monitored as the week goes on.",
